I've built a self-contained Alexa provider that registers a real Music Skill and answers the directive flow itself, so Alexa owns the queue (native metadata, transport, multi-room) instead of pushing one stream URL at a time. It builds on @alams154's auth and device discovery, which it credits.

It overlaps the existing alexa provider, so before I open a PR I want your call on shape: a separate ma_alexa provider, or fold the native-skill path into the existing alexa provider as a mode? It is running here against ten Echo devices. Code is at github.com/312-dev/ma-alexa-music-skill.

@marcelveldt @alams154

### What happened

On MA **2.9.2** (schema 31), pausing a Sonos **sync group** (`provider: sync_group`, built-in) flips the group player — and its queue — straight to `state: "idle"`. It never reports `paused`, even though the queue keeps its items and current track, and the group resumes fine via `players/cmd/play`. Plain (non-group) Sonos players report `paused` correctly on the same setup.

Captured live from the websocket ~4s after `players/cmd/pause` on the group:

```json
PLAYING: {"player_id":"syncgroup_nrchdx9k","display_name":"Down Stairs","state":"playing", ..., "type":"group","provider":"sync_group"}
PAUSED : {"player_id":"syncgroup_nrchdx9k","display_name":"Down Stairs","state":"idle","playback_state":"idle", ...}
QUEUE  : {"queue_id":"syncgroup_nrchdx9k","state":"idle","active":true,"items":61,"current":"Leif Shires - I Will Wait For You"}
```

### Why it matters

Any consumer keying UI or transport decisions off the reported state sees a paused group as "idle / nothing to act on" — a wall panel shows IDLE for paused music, and a naive "nothing playing → refuse transport" check refuses a resume that MA itself performs fine. The underlying Sonos zone does report paused,
